  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "When Kostya summons Barinov to the kitchen, Victor says goodbye to Elena until their date tomorrow. Before leaving the hotel, however, Elena gets a call from her friend Natasha and decides to stay for lunch in Barinov's restaurant.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Увы, ах, надо идти.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего без меня не могут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Ну, мне тоже пора. Да.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "До завтра. До завтра.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Алло. Да, Наташа.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "А я в Виктории, ага.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Который в отеле.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Давай, подъезжай скорее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Подъезжай, пообедаем, поболтаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Давай, я тебя жду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Жду тебя в ресторане.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "hostess",
      "t": "Здрасте, проходите пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Увы, ах, надо идти.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alas and alack, I have to go.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'alas' and 'ah' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего без меня не могут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They cannot do a single thing without me.' In Russian, the idea of 'nothing' flows naturally into 'can' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Ну, мне тоже пора. Да.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, it is time for me to go too. Yes.' The grammar packs 'come on' and 'yes' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "До завтра. До завтра.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Until tomorrow. Until tomorrow.' The meaning hinges on 'until' working together with 'tomorrow' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Алло. Да, Наташа.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello. Yes, Natasha.' In Russian, the idea of 'hello' flows naturally into 'Natasha' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "А я в Виктории, ага.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am at the Victor right now, yeah.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'and' and finishing on 'yeah'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Который в отеле.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The one inside the hotel.' Notice how Russian expresses 'who' and 'in the hotel' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Давай, подъезжай скорее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on over right now.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'come on' before landing on 'quickly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Подъезжай, пообедаем, поболтаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Drive up, we will have lunch and chat.' Elena brings out the contrast between 'come on over' and 'we will have lunch' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Давай, я тебя жду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, I am waiting for you.' Elena brings out the contrast between 'come on' and 'I' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "elena",
      "t": "Жду тебя в ресторане.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am waiting for you in the restaurant.' In Russian, the idea of 'am waiting for' flows naturally into 'restaurant' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "hostess",
      "t": "Здрасте, проходите пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, please come right in.' The meaning hinges on 'hi' working together with 'please' in spoken Russian. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
